Write kernel text directly under coLinux, without the poking mm.

__text_poke() patches live kernel text through a temporary mm: it maps the
target page at a user address, switches CR3 into that mm, writes through the
alias, and switches back. The alias exists because kernel text is read-only, so
it cannot be written at its own address.

Neither half of that holds for a cooperative guest. The image is mapped by the
host with _PAGE_RW and mark_rodata_ro()'s protection was never applied, so the
text is writable where it lives. And the switch cannot happen anyway:
use_temporary_mm() changes mm by writing CR3, and native_write_cr3() is a no-op
for a cooperative guest, because the host owns the address space and a guest
CR3 load would throw it away. So the mm switch does nothing, the guest stays on
its own tables, and the memcpy writes to the poking address -- which is mapped
only in the temporary mm and faults here.

That was the #PF at text_poke_memcpy+0xa, writing 0x00002aaaaaaabc0a (the
user-space poking address) with the guest still on init_mm, at 146 million
instructions in.

So when co_colinux_guest, write to addr directly and keep the memcmp that
verifies it. Same bytes the alias would have reached, none of the machinery
that cannot run.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c
@@ -2578,6 +2578,35 @@
 	 */
 	BUG_ON(!after_bootmem);
 
+	/*
+	 * A cooperative guest writes straight to the target.
+	 *
+	 * The whole apparatus below -- a temporary mm with the page aliased at a
+	 * user address, a CR3 switch into it, the write, a CR3 switch back --
+	 * exists for one reason: kernel text is mapped read-only, so it has to
+	 * be reached through a second, writable mapping. Under coLinux the image
+	 * is mapped by the host with _PAGE_RW and mark_rodata_ro()'s protection
+	 * was never applied, so the text is already writable at its own address.
+	 *
+	 * And the mechanism cannot run here regardless: use_temporary_mm()
+	 * switches mm by writing CR3, which native_write_cr3() suppresses for a
+	 * cooperative guest -- the host owns the address space and a guest CR3
+	 * load would discard it. So the switch is a no-op, the guest stays on
+	 * its own tables, and the memcpy lands on the poking address, which is
+	 * unmapped there. That is the #PF at text_poke_memcpy this replaces.
+	 *
+	 * Writing to addr directly reaches the same bytes the alias would have,
+	 * with the same verification kept.
+	 */
+	if (co_colinux_guest) {
+		local_irq_save(flags);
+		func(addr, src, len);
+		if (func == text_poke_memcpy)
+			BUG_ON(memcmp(addr, src, len));
+		local_irq_restore(flags);
+		return addr;
+	}
+
